.         
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4 * 
. 
.         * For the dependent variable, we construct a gender attitudes index, 
> DV_H1, to capture individual beliefs about gender roles in parenting and hous
> ehold responsibilities.This index is based on responses to three statements f
> rom the 2018 SOEP individual questionnaire: (1) "Children under 6 suffer if t
> heir mother works", (2) "Children under 3 suffer if their mother works", and 
> (3) "It's best if men and women work equally so they can share family and hou
> sehold responsibilities equally." Respondents rated their agreement with each
>  item on a scale from 1 ("Do not agree at all") to 7 ("Completely agree"). Fo
> r interpretability, the first two items were reverse-coded so that higher val
> ues consistently reflect more gender-equal attitudes. We then compute DV_H1 a
> s the average of the three items, resulting in a continuous index where highe
> r scores represent more progressive views about gender equality in the family
>  domain.
.         
.         *The independent variable is male_daughter, an interaction variable t
> o identicate the effect of being the father of a daughter. the positive value
> s of male_daughter indicates larger increase in the support of gender equalit
> y for men than for women having daughters. 
.         *We use a tobit regression since dependent variable is censored betwe
> en 1 and 7
.         *an additional note is that differently from the PAP we did not have 
> any religiousness measure in this data, hence we have omitted it from control
>  variables.
. 
.         tobit DV_H4 HasDaughter male male_daughter pglabnet_clean Age Younges
> tAge Employed_p pgisced11_clean pgisced11_p biokids MarriedStatus, ll(1) ul(7
> )

